# Batch files are parsed line by line by cmd.exe, and a label read with a
# trailing LF is not always the label that was written. A .cmd checked out
# with Unix endings can run its way past a `goto` and into the branch it was
# meant to skip, which is a failure mode with no error message at all.
#
# Everything else here is either read by bash on a Linux server (where CRLF
# is the mirror-image problem: `#!/bin/bash\r` is not an interpreter that
# exists) or compiled, so only the Windows launcher is pinned.
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer   text eol=lf
*.path    text eol=lf
